# [The problem]($heading.id('the-problem'))

A coding agent can write a thousand lines in an afternoon, and they will compile, pass their tests and look fine in a diff. The mistakes left in them are the ones that look fine too: a test that mocks the function it claims to check, an `except Exception: pass` around the call that matters, a `|| true` that keeps CI green, a second helper that does what the first one already did under a slightly different name.

Type checkers don't look for these. A reviewer skimming a large diff misses them. And the agent writes the same ones again in its next session, because nothing told it they were wrong.

zanity finds them. It reads every file in your repository with [tree-sitter](https://tree-sitter.github.io), runs every rule in one pass, and reports each finding with the fix, in a form an agent can act on without a human translating. The same code always gives the same findings, nothing leaves your machine, and a whole repository checks in moments, so it can run on every agent turn, every commit and every CI job.

```console
$ zanity check . --all
.github/workflows/ci.yml:6:7: warning [relaxed-check] '- run: pytest || true' turns a failing command into a passing one, so a problem it should catch passes.
.github/workflows/ci.yml:8:9: warning [relaxed-check] 'continue-on-error: true' lets this CI step fail without failing the build, so a problem it should catch passes.
src/billing.py:12:5: warning [generic-catch] 'except Exception' catches every exception, including the ones this code can't handle.
src/billing.py:12:5: warning [swallowed-error] This error handler does nothing, so the failure disappears silently.
src/billing.py:18:26: warning [vague-error] 'invalid input' doesn't say which input or value failed, or what was expected.
tests/test_billing.py:9:2: warning [test-double] 'patch' replaces real behaviour with a stand-in, so the test can pass while the real code is broken.
tests/test_billing.py:12:5: warning [call-verification] 'assert_called_with' checks how the code was called rather than what it did, so a change that keeps the behaviour breaks the test.
tests/test_billing.py:17:5: warning [polling-loop] This loop polls with a sleep, so the test's speed and outcome depend on timing.
tests/test_billing.py:18:9: warning [sleep-in-test] 'time.sleep' makes this test wait on the clock, which slows the suite and hides timing bugs.
tests/test_billing.py:19:12: warning [network-in-test] 'requests.get' makes a real network request, so the test depends on a service being up and answering the same way every time.
```

Every finding above comes from the examples on this page.

# [What it removes]($heading.id('what-it-removes'))

zanity is organised around classes of error, not style. Each class below is something agents produce often, something that survives tests and review, and something a rule can settle from the code alone.

## [Tests that can't fail]($heading.id('tests-that-cant-fail'))

The most expensive agent mistake is a green test suite that checks nothing. When asked to make tests pass, an agent will mock the code under test, assert on how a mock was called, or catch any exception at all.

```python
@patch("billing.charge")
def test_refund_returns_the_charge(charge):
    refund(order)
    charge.assert_called_with(order.total)
```

This test passes whatever `refund` does with the money. zanity reports the patch of the project's own code (`test-double`), the check of how the mock was called rather than what happened (`call-verification`), fakes that branch on their arguments and so reimplement what they replace (`branching-double`), assertions that cannot fail (`constant-test-assertion`), `pytest.raises(Exception)` and its cousins (`broad-expected-error`), `assertTrue(a == b)` that fails without showing either value (`opaque-check`), and tests skipped with no condition (`skipped-test`).

## [Tests that pass by luck]($heading.id('tests-that-pass-by-luck'))

A flaky test teaches everyone, people and agents alike, to rerun until green. These are the usual causes, and each is found in the test's code before it ever flakes:

```python
def test_export_job_completes():
    start_export()
    while not export_done():
        time.sleep(0.5)
    assert requests.get(f"{API}/exports/1").json()["state"] == "done"
```

`sleep-in-test`, `polling-loop`, `network-in-test`, `database-in-test`, `filesystem-in-test`, `process-in-test`, `stdin-in-test`, `nondeterministic-test` (randomness or the current time) and `shared-state-in-test` (environment variables, the working directory, globals). Give zanity your runner's JUnit report and `slow-test` names the tests that take too long.

## [Failures that disappear]($heading.id('failures-that-disappear'))

Agents are rewarded for code that doesn't crash, so they wrap things in handlers that make the crash go away. The bug stays; only the evidence goes.

```python
try:
    sync_invoices(account)
except Exception:
    pass

if amount < 0:
    raise ValueError("invalid input")
```

`swallowed-error` and `generic-catch` report the first; `return-in-finally`, `unawaited-call` and `empty-block` find the other ways a failure goes unseen. The second raises, but its message names none of the values that failed, so whoever reads the log can't find the problem: `vague-error`, `cryptic-error` and `unconstructive-error` report messages that don't say what failed, in plain words, and what to do about it.

## [Quality gates quietly switched off]($heading.id('quality-gates-quietly-switched-off'))

Faced with a check it can't pass, an agent will often weaken the check instead. zanity reads your build, lint and CI configuration as well as your code:

```yaml
- run: pytest || true
- run: mypy src
  continue-on-error: true
```

`relaxed-check` reports `|| true`, `continue-on-error: true`, `--exit-zero`, `strict = false` and the like, and in source the comments and attributes that silence other tools: `# noqa`, `// eslint-disable`, `//nolint`, `#[allow(...)]`, `@SuppressWarnings`. `ungated-release` and `ungated-deployment` report a GitHub Actions job that publishes a package or deploys without the tests having passed first.

## [Duplication and drift across sessions]($heading.id('duplication-and-drift-across-sessions'))

Each agent session starts with no memory of the last one, so it writes a new helper rather than finding the old one, and names the same idea a new way. Over weeks a codebase grows three of everything.

`structural-twins` finds functions in different files whose bodies differ only in names and values. `name-drift` finds one concept spelled several ways, such as `order_total` and `total_order`. `duplicate-name`, `dead-symbol`, `dead-parameter`, `passthrough-wrapper`, `single-impl-abstraction` and `cyclic-import` find the leftovers of code that was added and never joined up. Give the project a `[vocabulary]` in `zanity.toml` and `forbidden-term` and `non-canonical-term` hold every name to the words you settled on, with `--fix` renaming them.

## [Security hazards]($heading.id('security-hazards'))

The shortcuts that get a demo working: `hardcoded-secret`, `sql-built-from-strings`, `shell-command`, `tls-verification-disabled`, `unsafe-deserialization`, `secret-in-log`, `weak-hash`, and `eval` and `exec` (`forbidden-call`). Each traces to an entry in zanity's engineering error catalogue.

## [Code without guard rails]($heading.id('code-without-guard-rails'))

For code that must not fail, `--production` adds NASA's Power of Ten: `unbounded-loop` for a `while True` with no way out, `recursion` across files, `assertion-density` for functions with fewer than two assertions that could catch a bug, and `assertion-side-effect` for assertions that change what they check. Assertions that cannot fail, such as restating a type, don't count.

## [What structure can't settle]($heading.id('what-structure-cant-settle'))

Some mistakes need judgement: a function named for something other than what it does, a comment describing behaviour the code no longer has, a retry of an operation that isn't safe to repeat, writes that leave data half updated if one fails. With `--infer`, zanity asks a model a yes or no question about each function where no deterministic rule already answered, and caches the answer against the function's source, so unchanged code is never asked twice. It is off unless you ask for it, and you choose the server, including one on your own machine. See [the guide]($link.page('guide').ref('judging-what-structure-cant-settle-with---infer')).

[Every rule]($link.page('rules')) says what it flags, how severe it is and how to fix it.

# [Built for the agent loop]($heading.id('built-for-the-agent-loop'))

A linter written for people prints a wall of text and trusts the reader to use judgement. zanity is written for the agent in the loop as much as the person reviewing it.

- **Output an agent can act on.** `--agent` reports in about 1% of the bytes of the terminal report: the totals, the command to run next, and each finding's line and its own fix, with `[--fix]` on those zanity can make itself. It is on by default under Claude Code, and `ZANITY_AGENT=1` turns it on for any other agent.
```console
$ zanity check . --agent
zanity: 0 errors, 12 warnings in 3/3 files.
Next: fix this displayed batch, rerun the full check with --fail-on warning. This is a selected batch. Resolve the displayed findings in code. If the code is correct, make that correctness explicit enough for the rule to verify; never suppress the finding, disable its rule or exclude its path.

swallowed-error [warning] 1 in 1 file: Handle the error, log it with context, or let it propagate.
  src/billing.py
    12:5 This error handler does nothing.

call-verification [warning] 1 in 1 file: Assert on the result, or the state the code leaves behind; check a call only where the call is the behaviour, such as a message sent to another system.
  tests/test_billing.py
    12:5 'assert_called_with' checks how the code was called rather than what it did.
```

- **No way out but fixing it.** The agent report tells the agent that when its code is correct, it must make that correctness explicit enough for the rule to verify, never suppress the finding, disable the rule or exclude the path. `relaxed-check` catches it silencing other tools instead.
- **Safe fixes.** `--fix` makes only changes that can't alter what the code does. Where only a person knows something, such as why an assertion holds, it leaves a `TODO` rather than guessing.
- **Fast enough for every turn.** One static binary, one pass over the syntax trees, and a cache that parses only what changed. `--watch` checks again on every save, so an agent sees its mistakes while it is still in the file.
- **Adopt it on an existing codebase.** Capture a baseline of today's findings, and `--new-only` fails only on what the agent adds, so the backlog doesn't block the work and can be paid down in bounded batches with `--triage`.
- **The same findings in your editor.** `zanity lsp` shows every finding as a diagnostic with its fix as a code action, so the person reviewing sees what the agent saw.

# [Where it fits]($heading.id('where-it-fits'))

Keep your formatter, your linter and your type checker. They check that code is written well in its language. zanity checks whether the code and its tests can be trusted: that a test would fail if the behaviour broke, that a failure would be seen and explained, that the checks guarding the code are still switched on, and that the codebase isn't quietly growing two of everything.

It checks Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, Rust, Java, C#, Swift, Lua and Zig, with every rule running on every language where it means something. It is a single binary with no runtime to install, and without `--infer` it never touches the network.

# [Try it]($heading.id('try-it'))

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/benomahony/zanity/main/install.sh | sh
zanity check .                     # see what it finds
zanity check . --fix               # apply the fixes that can't change behaviour
zanity init                        # write a zanity.toml with every setting explained
zanity check . --fail-on warning   # what pre-commit and CI should run
```

[Getting started]($link.page('guide')) covers Windows, pre-commit, GitHub Actions, baselines and the editor.
